# Phase 3 / cross-vendor validation
# Captured 2026-07-29 on AWS EC2 a1.metal (<instance-id redacted>, eu-central-1,
# terminated immediately after this capture -- bare-metal Graviton1,
# Annapurna Labs SoC, 16x Cortex-A72, kernel 6.8.0-1061-aws).
#
# Purpose: determine whether the GICv3/NISV KVM boot hang found on Jetson
# Orin Nano (Tegra234, Cortex-A78AE -- see docs/orin-port.md risk register,
# docs/findings.md 2026-07-28 "root-cause KVM boot hang" entry) is
# Tegra234-specific or a general real-hardware/KVM limitation. AWS a1.metal
# was chosen because it fits this AWS account's 32-vCPU quota (c7g.metal
# needs 64 vCPU and was quota-blocked); it is a DIFFERENT vendor (Annapurna
# Labs, not NVIDIA) and a DIFFERENT core generation (Cortex-A72, not
# Cortex-A78AE) from Orin -- if the same hang reproduces here, that is
# strong evidence the defect is general, not silicon-specific.
#
# Command (identical machine/accel/cpu shape to the Orin Nano KVM attempt
# in scripts/orin/launch-qnx-on-orin.sh, minus the tap/bridge networking
# which was not set up for this bare diagnostic run):
#
#   sudo timeout 60 qemu-system-aarch64 -machine virt,gic-version=3 \
#     -cpu host -enable-kvm -smp 2 -m 1G \
#     -drive file=disk-qemu,if=none,id=drv0,format=raw \
#     -device virtio-blk-device,drive=drv0 -kernel ifs.bin -nographic \
#     -serial file:boot-kvm.log -display none -no-reboot -monitor none
#
# Same ifs.bin + disk-qemu as the Orin Nano tests (sha256 recorded at
# transfer time: ifs.bin=92868b2f116f17c06d26585b9618682076913d17491bdeb22c4a0392d43dd745,
# disk-qemu=fd2ee67d46f5e83fea4648f6eb9530b4dd5c83d83f10d58a12ef6a63f5c07bde
# -- matches the qnx-safety-vm/output/ artifacts used throughout this repo).
#
# Result: QEMU_EXIT=124 (killed by the 60s timeout wrapper, not a QEMU
# crash/error -- run-kvm.log contained only the timeout-kill SIGTERM
# message). Serial log below is the ENTIRE captured output for the full
# 60 seconds.
#
# Host dmesg during this run showed a healthy KVM init (for reference,
# NOT captured from a hang state, this is boot-time host dmesg):
#   kvm [1]: IPA Size Limit: 44 bits
#   kvm [1]: vgic-v2@f0020000
#   kvm [1]: GIC system register CPU interface enabled
#   kvm [1]: vgic interrupt IRQ9
#   kvm [1]: Hyp mode initialized successfully
# Note the host's own physical GIC reports as vgic-v2 in this line -- this
# describes the Graviton1 host's own interrupt controller init, not
# necessarily what interface KVM exposes to a gic-version=3 guest (the
# earlier bare vGIC smoke test on this same instance, -machine
# virt,gic-version=3,accel=kvm with -kernel /dev/null, ran the full 8s with
# zero errors -- vGIC creation itself succeeds here, same as on Orin Nano).
#
# Honest framing: this is ONE run, not a repeated-measurement series (per
# this repo's own convention, contrast with the boot-time n=5 series in
# logs/sample-boot/*-boot-times-n5.txt). It is strong, real evidence for
# cross-vendor reproduction, not a statistically hardened claim.

